.\" Man page for alma-certify, the AlmaLinux hardware certification suite.
.\"
.\" Deliberately narrow. It names every command, the options that change what a run *claims*, and
.\" the files a reader has to know about, and it sends anyone after the full option list to --help.
.\" A man page that restates every flag drifts from the parser the first time somebody adds one, and
.\" a stale man page is worse than a short one.
.TH ALMA\-CERTIFY 1 "August 2026" "alma-certify" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
alma\-certify \- collect, validate, and benchmark hardware for AlmaLinux certification
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B alma\-certify
.RI [ global-option ...]
.I command
.RI [ option ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B alma\-certify
runs the hardware certification suite of the AlmaLinux Certification SIG. It gathers a hardware
inventory, runs functional validation tests, and measures benchmarks, then writes a versioned JSON
report and the raw artifacts behind it into a run directory. A run can be submitted to the AlmaLinux
certification web application over the network, or bundled as a tarball and uploaded by hand from a
machine with no route to it.
.PP
Run with no command at an interactive terminal,
.B alma\-certify
starts its guided full-screen interface. Every action it offers is also a command with options, so
nothing here needs a terminal: the suite is built to run unattended from a kickstart
.B %post
or a CI job, and never waits for input it was not given.
.PP
Supported on AlmaLinux 8, 9, and 10, on x86_64 and aarch64 only. ppc64le and s390x are not
supported: neither has a
.BR dmidecode (8)
build, and this suite reads SMBIOS to identify the machine it is certifying.
.PP
Certifying a machine needs bare metal. A run inside a virtual machine or a container is performed
and kept, but a whole-machine claim from a guest is never uploaded: the firmware, storage controller,
and network device it tested are the hypervisor's. A GPU passed through to a guest is the real
device, so
.B \-\-scope gpu
is submittable from there.
.PP
Every command needs root, because reading DMI, PCI configuration space, and SMART data does, and
because the run directory and the submission token are root's. Run without it at a terminal, the
suite offers to re-run itself under
.BR sudo (8)
and does so if you agree; with nothing to ask, it refuses and prints the command to run rather than
prompting into a closed stdin.
.B \-\-help
and
.B \-\-version
need nothing.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B collect
Collect the hardware inventory and nothing else.
.TP
.B validate
Run the validation tests.
.TP
.B benchmark
Run the benchmarks.
.TP
.B run
Collect, validate, and benchmark in one run.
.TP
.B resume
Continue a run that was interrupted, skipping the tests that already finished.
.TP
.B setup\-gpu
Install the NVIDIA driver and CUDA toolkit a GPU needs before it can be certified. Prints the exact
commands it will run and what they change, and asks before doing any of it.
.TP
.B tui
Start the guided interface explicitly.
.TP
.B runs
List the runs in the run directory, newest first, with what each one claims and whether it has been
submitted.
.TP
.B list
List the available tests.
.TP
.B report
Print a human-readable summary of a run.
.TP
.B bundle
Package a run as a tarball for manual upload.
.TP
.B register
Obtain a submission token from the server.
.TP
.B submit
Submit a run to the server.
.SH OPTIONS
The options below change what a run claims or where it goes, which is why they are here. For the
complete list, including test selection and the interactive test gate, see
.B alma\-certify
.I command
.BR \-\-help .
.TP
.BI \-\-config " FILE"
Read configuration from
.I FILE
instead of the system configuration file.
.TP
.B \-\-dev
Submit to the staging catalog at https://lumina.almalinux.dev rather than the production one at
https://catalog.almalinux.org. For developing against a pre\-release server;
.B \-\-server
overrides it.
.TP
.BI \-\-server " URL"
Base URL of the certification server. Overrides the configuration file, and is also read from
.BR $ALMA_CERTIFY_SERVER .
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-self\-signed
Accept the server's TLS certificate without verifying it, for a dev or staging catalog serving a
self-signed certificate. This accepts
.I any
certificate from whatever answers on that address and stops checking that the name matches, so use it
only against a server you know. Every run then reports on standard error that verification is off.
Also settable as
.B allow_self_signed
in the configuration file, for a machine that always talks to such a catalog. Where you have a copy
of the certificate, adding it to the host's trust store is the better answer and leaves this alone.
.TP
.BI \-\-run\-dir " DIR"
Base directory for run state.
.TP
.BI \-\-scope " KIND" [,KIND...]
Record the run as evidence about the named components and about nothing else, rather than about the
whole machine.
.B gpu
is the only kind accepted: a graphics card is the one component a virtual machine sees as the real
device, so a scoped run may be made in a guest, while certifying a machine needs bare metal. A
scoped run still records everything it collected, because the host is context a reviewer wants, and
the server will not create, link, or certify a system listing from it. Narrows which tests run as
well, unless
.B \-\-category
says otherwise.
.TP
.B \-\-pre\-release
Mark the hardware as unreleased. The submission is embargoed and withheld from public view.
.TP
.BI \-\-publish\-after " YYYY-MM-DD"
Earliest date the result may be published.
.TP
.B \-\-redact
Omit serial numbers and UUIDs from the report.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-epel
Do not install
.B epel\-release
or enable CodeReady Builder. Tests whose tools are not already present are skipped rather than
installed for.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-submit
Finish the run and stop, without offering to submit it.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-unsupported\-os
Run on a host that is not a supported AlmaLinux release without being asked to confirm. The result is
recorded as having run elsewhere and certifies nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the suite version and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/alma-certify/alma-certify.conf
System configuration: server URL, run directory, and defaults for the options above.
.TP
.I /var/cache/alma-certify/token.json
The submission token from
.BR "alma\-certify register" .
Mode 0600 in a 0700 directory: it authorizes uploads as you. Not in
.IR /etc ,
because it is a credential this machine fetches for itself and can fetch again, not configuration.
.TP
.I /var/lib/alma-certify/runs
Run state, one directory per run, holding the journal, the report, and the artifacts. Readable by
root only, because an inventory names the machine down to its serial numbers.
.TP
.I /usr/share/alma-certify
The suite itself, private to this package.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Everything that ran passed.
.TP
.B 1
The run finished and something it tested failed.
.TP
.B 2
The command could not do what was asked.
.TP
.B 3
The command line was wrong.
.TP
.B 4
The host is not a supported AlmaLinux release and the prompt to continue anyway was declined, or
.B submit
was given a run that cannot be uploaded: one made on another distribution, or a whole-machine run
made inside a virtual machine. Its own code rather than a usage error, so a script driving the suite
can tell being blocked apart from hardware that ran and failed.
.SH SEE ALSO
The suite's own documentation, in
.IR /usr/share/doc/alma-certify/ ,
covers the report schema and what each test measures.
.SH BUGS
Report bugs at
.UR https://github.com/AlmaLinux/alma-certify/issues
.UE .
